%I A141399 #74 Aug 25 2026 19:17:48
%S A141399 1,2,3,5,8,9,14,15,20,24,35,80,125,224,384,440,539,714,1715,2079,2400,
%T A141399 3024,4374,9800,12375,123200,194480,633555
%N A141399 Positive integers k such that the distinct primes that divide k or k+1 form a set of consecutive primes. In other words, k is included if and only if k*(k+1) is contained in sequence A073491.
%C A141399 The smallest prime in the set of consecutive primes is always 2, since k*(k+1) is even.
%C A141399 No further terms thru 5*10^8. - _Ray Chandler_, Jun 24 2009
%C A141399 a(29) > 2.29*10^25, if it exists. - _Giovanni Resta_, Nov 30 2019
%C A141399 This sequence contains k such that rad(k*(k+1)) is in A055932, where rad = A007947. - _Michael De Vlieger_, Jul 13 2024
%C A141399 The first comment implies that one could replace A073491 in the second definition by the more specific A055932. Equivalently, these are integers k such that the number of distinct prime factors of k*(k+1), A059957(k) = A001222(k^2+k), is equal to the index of the largest prime factor of k*(k+1), A252489(k) = A000720(A074399(k)). - _M. F. Hasler_, Mar 31 2026
%C A141399 From _Ken Clements_, Aug 06 2026: (Start)
%C A141399 Let p(r) be the r-th prime and let P(r) = 2*3*5...p(r) = A002110(r). A118478(r) is the least positive integer, k, such that P(r) divides k*(k+1). Therefore, if k is a term of this sequence with omega(k*(k+1)) = r, then k >= A118478(r). Thus A118478 provides a lower floor for possible terms of this sequence where omega(k*(k+1)) = r.
%C A141399 A002072(r) is the largest integer, k, for which both k and k+1 have no prime factor greater than p(r). Since a term, k, of this sequence with omega(k*(k+1)) = r satisfies rad(k*(k+1)) = P(r), both k and k+1 are p(r)-smooth. Consequently, k <= A002072(r). Every term, k, producing k*(k+1) having order r = omega(k*(k+1)), must therefore lie in the squeeze interval A118478(r) <= k <= A002072(r).
%C A141399 The table below places the known terms, k, grouped by the distribution of equal values of r = omega(k*(k+1)) given by _Michael De Vlieger_, within these squeeze intervals. An exhaustive Pell-equation search through r = 18 produced only the known terms for 1 <= r <= 8, and no terms for 9 <= r <= 18. The established sequence values show A118478(r) > A002072(r) for 14 <= r <= 27, so the squeeze interval is empty for those orders. It follows from these two results that any new term of this sequence would produce r = omega(k*(k+1)) of at least 28. That product, would therefore be divisible by P(28) which is approximately 2.6*10^42.
%C A141399 Conjecture: A118478(r) > A002072(r) for every r > 13. Together with the exhaustive search for 9 <= r = omega(k*(k+1)) <= 13, this conjecture would imply that this sequence has no further terms. (End)
%e A141399 20 is factored as 2^2 * 5^1. 21 is factored as 3^1 * 7^1. Since the distinct primes that divide 20 and 21 (which are 2,3,5,7) form a set of consecutive primes, then 20 is in the sequence.
%e A141399 From _Michael De Vlieger_, Jul 13 2024: (Start)
